In article <Cq0w6L.2r8@viewlogic.com>, gwu@everest.viewlogic.com (Gary Wu) writes:

>  I have an external Seagate 2.1G disk that I would like to 
>  attach to a DEC 3000 Model 500 running OSF/1 v2.0.
>
> [ some stuff deleted [
>
>  Now, how can I create the correct /dev/rz files for it and
>  newfs it ?  The /dev/rz files created with MAKEDEV seem to
>  work only for SCSI_A (internal scsi) devices.

Simple answer - Add 8 to the target number you select for the
disk.  This will give one of:

	rz8, rz9, rz10, rz11, rz12, rz13, rz14, rz15

Use MAKEDEV to create the appropriate set of special
device files.  Or just create all of them.  It save
having to worry about it the next time.

Longer answer - See the system management guide for instructions
on how to update the configuration for the addition of new
devices.  This will probably suggest making a copy of the
current vmunix and then copying genvmunix over it.  Boot
that and run doconfig to build the new configuration file.

The kernel genvmunix has all the likely drivers and devices
built it to it.  Doconfig will use sizer to determine what
devices were found and build an appropriate configuration
file.  From this a configuration specific kernel will be
built which can be booted.  Then watch the boot messages
to see what name the Seagate is found under.  Create the
special device files as appropriate.

Alternatively, you can edit the configuration file by hand.
The two built-in SCSI adapters are asc0 and asc1 on tcds0.
The full expansion for the configuration for all possible
devices on those two SCSI channels is at the end of the
follow-up.

If you're likely to be adding another SCSI adapter to the
TURBOchannel it may be wise to just go ahead and add all
the necessary lines.  A couple of days ago I built a kernel
with the minimum configuration file to match the configuration
and a configuration with all the possible SCSI adapters and
devices in it.  The difference in size of the two kernels was
80 KB.

To get a clue what all the SCSI adapters and devices will
look like, look at the GENERIC configuration file.

bus             tcds0   at *   vector tcdsintr
controller      asc0    at *   vector ascintr
controller      asc1    at *   vector ascintr
#
device disk             rz0     at asc0         drive 0
device disk             rz1     at asc0         drive 8
device disk             rz2     at asc0         drive 16
device disk             rz3     at asc0         drive 24
device disk             rz4     at asc0         drive 32
device disk             rz5     at asc0         drive 40
device disk             rz6     at asc0         drive 48
device disk             rz7     at asc0         drive 56
device disk             rz8     at asc1         drive 64
device disk             rz9     at asc1         drive 72
device disk             rz10    at asc1         drive 80
device disk             rz11    at asc1         drive 88
device disk             rz12    at asc1         drive 96
device disk             rz13    at asc1         drive 104
device disk             rz14    at asc1         drive 112
device disk             rz15    at asc1         drive 120
#
device tape             tz0     at asc0         drive 0
[ Its just like the disk list with "tape" replacing "disk"
  and "tz" replacing "rz". ]

