Re: receiver-makes-it-right

The issue about protocol extension is correct, but is not unique
to the DCE/NCS data representation--if you change what the sender
sends, then the receiver needs to know what to do with it.  The
name receiver-makes-it-right seems to imply unlimited flexibility
requirements on the receiver ("OK, PDP-10 input with 36 bit words,
time to make it right"), whereas in actuality the sender has
a small set of canonical data representation choices to make,
and tags the data with the choice it makes.  The receiver has to
handle these choices, but no others.  The choices allow most systems
to avoid data conversions when sender and receiver are similar,
which is a small win for simple data types and a bigger win for
complex types such as floating point (and soon to be supported
multibyte characters).  "Unusual" architectures (e.g. PDP-10)
still have to do conversions before transmission.

Doug Hartman


