        Yes, it can be done.  HP's ORB+ is one highly advertised example, but
        in addition to the information previously posted, there is in fact an
        effort underway to define an "OODCE", and multiple vendors now have
        projects of this type underway.

>>2. How might this be done? 
        One of the most straightforward possibilities is to create abstraction 
wrappers
        to DCE services and "bolt" the OO layer onto DCE.

>>3. Why would anyone ever want to use DCE? (I'm exaggerating this question 
        for comedic effect :-)
        In point of fact, the reasons are twofold (and I am not exaggerating):
        NCR, now AT&T Global Information Solutions has explored this problem 
space
        over the last year from two perspectives.  As product manager for our OO 
foundation         product, I offer you the consolidated response:
        #1 - the customer perspective; key accounts that we have worked with 
over the 
        past year, wishing to implement global or large-scale systems have found 
that                   CORBA lacks the performance and robustness of features 
and services they                      required to implement commercial 
systems...and that in fact, it looked like it would         be several years 
before enough services were incorporated into CORBA to catch
        it up to the depth and breadth of functions and services provided in DCE 
(horrid
        though it may be to implement on).
        #2 - the vendor perspective; given the feedback we received from these 
accounts,
        and their stated preference to implement a lighter weight, higher 
performance
        request broker over DCE, we decided to work with them to modify a C++ 
ORB
        (this because it performed 8 orders of magnitude faster than the similar 
CORBA
        implementation on their systems).  
        This helped us satisfy the customer's performance desire.  When 
examining what         they found compelling in DCE, we decided that it would be 
economically unfeasible         to try and replicate (either on our own or with 
a consortia of vendors) the entire 
        breadth and depth of DCE just to say it had been done in a "pure" object
        construct.

         So, for all of its faults, DCE appears to have some services and 
functions that
         commercial users of large scale, distributed computing systems find
         necessary, and implementing a object layer (in our case distributing 
C++ across
         the net on top of DCE) provides a means for object programmers to use 
an ORB
         and issue requests for DCE services through abstractions.




