Hr. Thomas Keller (WST) (keller@emndev.siemens.co.at) wrote:
: o Which transport protocols are currently supported by DCE RPC ?
:   (i am sure for TCP and UDP, DEC's NCP (DECNET) may be)

I think those are the only ones.  (Microsoft's version supports some
other, LAN Manager-oriented protocols, but I suspect you didn't have
those in mind.)

: o If an client calls a service for the first time the rpcb 
:   daemon is involved. (for obtaining the transport endpoint )
:   I think, the rpcb service forwards the call to the related 
:   server. What's going on, if the used transport protocol is 
:   connection oriented ( like TCP)? The easyest way to find out the
:   client and server sides runtime activitys for the connection
:   establishment before the first call is to make a netwok 
:   activity trace. (But that isnt possible for everyone :-))

The client-side RPC runtime makes an RPC to the RPCD to obtain the
server's endpoint.  (Since the RPCD is listening for RPCs on a
well-known port, there's no infinite recursion.)  The net result is
that the traffic you'd see is: transport connect to RPCD,
call/response message pair for the RPC to the RPCD, transport connect
to the server, call/response message pair for the RPC to the server.

: o How long a connection between a client and a server lives. 
:   I hav'nt found any hint about the connection lifetime... 
:   Is there any way to control this?

I don't recall how long it is--on the order of a small number of
minutes or less, I think.  And I don't think there's a way to control
it.  Is there some reason you'd want to?

: o How is a special call semantik realized by the rpc-runtime? 
:   In my mind there are two ways possible.
:   1st is to set up the transport protocol parameters 
:   (for example a at-least-once semantik with an 
:   connectionless proto may be guaranteed by setting the retransmit 
:   timer greater then the timeout value)
:   2nd is to support this by special RPC-protocol features...
:   (This is the requirement to add future semantics how exactly once)

At most once, regardless of the choice of transport.
