>Do you mean DCE doesn't handle cells which span multiple networks of different
>subnet address and/or the networks are separated by router/bridge/gateway ? 
>We're using both ethernet and FDDI, and also DNS :-(.  The networks here are
>'partitioned' into different subnets.  I'm afraid we're facing similar problems
>as yours.  

>Will IBM fix these problems soon ?

Here's how it works.  Let's say you have an RS/6000 with both an ethernet 
and a FDDI adapter. Both have been configured for TCP/IP and have different 
IP addresses.  Let's also say that this machine is your cell directory 
server and security sever.  When you configure the machine for DCE services 
the configuration command looks at the current hardware & TCP/IP configuration
and will store the IP address (not the host name) of both adapter cards in 
your CDS.  So, when an application wants to find a binding handle for this 
machine, CDS will randomly pick either the FDDI address or the ethernet 
address.  (Actually it passes both addresses back to the client, but
the order is random.)  Now let's assume you wrote your application in such 
a way as to not care about which address was first, but you want to send 
RPC's over FDDI instead of ethernet and the FDDI & ethernet subnets are 
not routed or bridged.  If a client on the ethernet side is given a
binding handle with the FDDI address first your application will hang 
because you can't get there from here! - I hope this makes sense... 

Anyway IBM is NOT going to fix this because they believe it is up to 
the application to decide which binding handle to use.  You have a few 
work arounds however.

1.)  Set up a router which accesses both networks and configure your 
network clients to know about the router.  This will route the ethernet 
traffic thru the router to the FDDI subnet and back so that the servers 
and clients can reach both networks. Of course, you loose all control of 
where your going to and from.

2.)  There is an environment variable RPC_UNSUPPORTED_NETIFS which will 
turn off one or more adapters from DCE control.  So, 
RPC_UNSUPPORTED_NETIFS="en0 tr0" will turn off DCE for ethernet and 
tokenring.  Of course, the router will be needed to bridge everything
together if you want FDDI to get the traffic from ethernet.  You will 
also have to stop using DNS so that a gethostbyname will not confuse any 
of the applications which do not rely solely on DCE CDS and so that any 
clients configured after the server is set up will not try to go to 
the ethernet address.

> Other problems have to do with time services.  If your systems clock is off
> by more that 5 minutes from you time server DCE will not come up properly.
> We found this one when we upgraded a 580 to a 590 (the DTS server) and the
> system clock was reset to 1969!.  We called IBM (on a Saturday, when they
> don't support DCE!)  and they said that if we brought up the client DTS
> that as soon as the time differential had elapsed that the system would be 
> back up.  I then explained that I did not have 30 years to wait for the
> clock to resyncronize!!!  We brought everything down and reset the clocks
> and lucked out!!!
> 
> Well, I could give you a lot more horror stories but why spoil all the fun!

Anyway, may I know those horror stories too ?  ;-)  Sorry to bother you so
much and thanks for your help !!

I'll try to send them has they come up.  One that does come to mind is that
the userid's in security server are IP specific, so if you move an aplication
you have to rebuild your security server to know about the users in the new 
