f89-kam@alv.nada.kth.se (Klas Mellbourn) writes:


: I've been using a DEC alpha via modem for a while but now we've got
: one installed in our office (Yay!). I have never seriously used a DEC
: keyboard before so my question is: which programs actually use the
: Compose Character shift keys?

None at all. The operating system itself (keyboard driver or however this 
is organized on DEC machines) uses the key to generate input of characters 
which are in the character set but not on your keyboard. This applies to 
accented characters, other composed characters, and other special characters. 
The input method is to press "Compose" first, then two other keys to combine 
the character to be input. Examples:
Compose a "  gives you an � (a diaeresis / a umlaut)
Compose e '  gives you an � (e acute / e accent d'aigu)
Compose a e  gives you an � (ae ligature, used in Danish, Latin, and sometimes 
                             in English)
Compose L -  gives you a � (the pound character)
Any decent program should accept these characters like any other. There 
is one strange thing with this key on at least some DEC machines. It only 
works if pressed in conjunction with the space key (what a queer idea!), 
then the other two keys.
This key is extremely useful whenever a character beyond the limited ASCII 
character set has to be typed.

