If you are using AIX DCE, you will find in its release notes, telling you how
to use the tools that comes with its AIX DCE release for setting up DNS server.

If I remember correctly (I used it a while ago), here are what you do:

1. in one of machine in the cell:

	$ cdscp show cell /.:/ as dns > a_file
	$ cdscp show clearinghouse /.:/\* >> a_file

2. ftp a_file to the DNS server machine

3. if the DNS server machine has not been installed with DCE software, do it.

4. on the DNS server machine, use "smit". Under DCE menus, there  is one for
   DNS setup. Select it. Enter a_file name and DNS server database file name.
   The script will take the information from a_file and add to the DNS database
   file.

5. Make sure that the machine that runs CDS server in any cell that will talk to
   DNS to get the cell information, starts gdad (using smit to start it) and its
   /etc/resolv.conf uses the right DNS server.

The IBM tool is easy to use. The only drawback is that you have to install DCE
software on the DNS server, which may well be a machine that you don't want to
have anything to do with DCE.

